A backend language requires a Web Server environment to receive website input. While any general-purpose programming language can run on a server, a select few dominate modern web development. [1, 2, 3] 
Here is every major backend language and exactly how each handles user input (via URL strings, HTML forms, and API payloads). [4, 5, 6] 
------------------------------
## 1. Java (Java EE / Spring Boot)
Java utilizes standard Web Servlets. In modern applications, developers use the Spring Boot framework, which uses annotations to bind data. [7, 8] 

* @RequestParam: Captures URL query variables (e.g., ?id=5).
* @PathVariable: Extracts data from a RESTful URL path (e.g., /users/{id}).
* @RequestBody: Automatically parses raw incoming JSON or XML payloads into a Java object.
* @ModelAttribute: Maps traditional HTML multipart or form-urlencoded POST data to a class.
* @RequestHeader & @CookieValue: Extracts specific HTTP headers and tracking cookies. [9, 10, 11, 12, 13] 

## 2. Ruby (Ruby on Rails)
Ruby on Rails uses a highly unified architecture. It funnels absolutely all incoming user data—regardless of whether it came from a GET, POST, or JSON payload—into a single hash object. [14] 

* params[]: The master method. You call params[:id] to read a query variable, a form text box, or a JSON key seamlessly.
* request.headers[]: Reads metadata sent by the browser.
* cookies[]: Accesses cookies stored on the client side. [15, 16] 

## 3. C# (.NET Core / ASP.NET)
Microsoft's backend ecosystem uses strict typing and Model Binding to map data directly to function parameters or objects.

* [FromQuery]: Pulls parameters directly from the URL query string.
* [FromRoute]: Pulls parameters out of the clean URL path.
* [FromBody]: Parses a raw JSON request body into a typed C# class.
* [FromForm]: Reads traditional HTML form submissions.
* [FromHeader]: Extracts specific HTTP request headers. [17, 18, 19, 20, 21] 

## 4. Go (Golang)
Go relies heavily on its powerful, built-in standard library (net/http), though frameworks like Gin are also common. [22, 23, 24, 25] 

* r.URL.Query().Get(): Extracts variables out of the URL path query.
* r.FormValue(): Reads key-value pairs submitted by standard HTML forms.
* json.NewDecoder(r.Body).Decode(): Unmarshals raw JSON payload streams directly into a Go struct.
* r.Header.Get(): Reads HTTP header fields. [26, 27, 28] 

## 5. Rust (Actix-web / Axum)
Rust focuses on safety and speed, using specific type structures called "extractors" inside function arguments to parse website data. [29] 

* Query<T>: Extracts and validates URL query data into a defined structure.
* Path<T>: Extracts variables directly out of the route path string.
* Json<T>: Automatically deserializes and type-checks a JSON payload body.
* Form<T>: Handles traditional URL-encoded HTML form data submissions. [30] 

## 6. Summary: PHP, Python, & JavaScript (Recap)
As detailed previously, these environments use their own dedicated mechanics:

* PHP: Uses global system arrays ($_GET, $_POST, $_FILES) or the raw stream wrapper (php://input).
* Python: Relies on framework request objects like request.args (Flask) or request.POST (Django).
* JavaScript (Node.js): Utilizes the req stream object in raw HTTP, or req.query and req.body in frameworks like Express.js. [31, 32, 33, 34, 35] 

